{"content":"⚠️ **IMPORTANT: "Fail loudly" fires in production, not in CI — and it kills the whole run**

The router in `_assistantMessageEventJson` sends every `AssistantMessageEvent` that is not one of the 7 text/thinking subtypes into `_callStreamEventJson`, whose `default:` arm throws `StateError('untriaged AssistantMessageEvent: …')`. Because the outer dispatch is an `is`-chain (not an exhaustive sealed-type switch), the compiler will *not* flag a new subtype added to the sealed `AssistantMessageEvent` hierarchy — the throw is the only signal, and it fires at runtime.

What happens then:

1. `StreamJsonWriter.handleEvent` throws.
2. `Agent._processEvent` awaits listeners without isolation, so the exception propagates out of the loop into `Agent._handleRunFailure` — the entire headless run is converted to `stopReason: error` ("Bad state: untriaged AssistantMessageEvent…"), exit code 1, mid-conversation. A serialization gap in an *output projection* takes down the run itself.

The doc comment above `_assistantMessageEventJson` says "the encoder test enumerates every `AssistantMessageEvent` subtype so a new one cannot slip through unserialized" — but that tripwire doesn't exist. `test/cli/stream_json_test.dart` has the `hasLength(13)` tripwire for `AgentEvent` only; the `AssistantMessageEvent` tests cover each current subtype individually, and nothing breaks when a 13th subtype is added without touching the encoder.

Two complementary fixes (either is acceptable, both is best):

```dart
// 1. Test-side tripwire mirroring the AgentEvent one:
expect(assistantMessageEventSamples, hasLength(12)); // bump on every new subtype

// 2. Runtime degradation in StreamJsonWriter.handleEvent:
Future<void> handleEvent(AgentEvent event, CancelToken? cancelToken) async {
  final String? line;
  try {
    line = streamJsonEventLine(event);
  } on Object {
    return; // a projection hiccup must never kill the run
  }
  if (line != null) _emit(line);
}
```

(2) also covers `jsonEncode` throwing on non-finite doubles (`UsageCost` values are `double`; a provider-reported NaN/Infinity would take the same path today).
